    description:
      "First described by Clance and Imes in high-achieving women, imposter phenomenon is the persistent belief that one's success is undeserved. A systematic review of 62 studies (14,161 participants) found prevalence from 9% to 82% depending on the instrument, comorbidity with depression and anxiety, links to impaired performance and burnout — and no published treatment evaluations. Reviews of medical trainees put prevalence at 22–47%, roughly twice as high in women. What is associated with less of it in the data: mentorship and time in role. A workshop that measured it and taught mitigation strategies was rated useful by 96% of first-year medical students, but that is satisfaction, not outcome data. The boundary: everything here is observational.",
    action: "Take the Clance Impostor Phenomenon Scale once, tell one trusted mentor or peer your score and what triggers it, and keep a dated list of concrete things you did well that you reread before evaluations.",
