    description:
      "Holland's theory classifies people and work environments into Realistic, Investigative, Artistic, Social, Enterprising and Conventional types and predicts better outcomes when they match. A meta-analysis spanning 60 years (60 studies, about 568 correlations) found interests predict performance and persistence in work and school, and that person–environment congruence indices predict performance more strongly than interest scores alone; a follow-up re-analysis confirmed the congruence effect. Interest–major congruence predicted college persistence and timely graduation in a large longitudinal study; interest fit is a consistent correlate of job satisfaction in meta-analysis; and interests measured at the end of high school predicted income and life outcomes ten years later over and above IQ and personality. The boundary: fit is a probability shifter, not a guarantee, and interests keep developing.",
